<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deir el-Bersha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deir el-Bersha is an ancient cemetery on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>, opposite Mallawi. Its rock-cut tombs belonged mainly to the nomarchs of the Hare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> in the Middle Kingdom. The tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djehutyhotep">Djehutyhotep</a> contains a famous scene of a colossal statue being dragged on a sledge by teams of workers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Deir el-Bersha is a Coptic village in the Minya Governorate, south of Antinoöpolis. Its name may preserve the ancient Egyptian Pr-šs, House of Travertine, through Coptic. The best-known part of the necropolis is the elite cemetery on the North Hill, on the north flank of Wadi Nakhla, where the governors of the Hare nome, the 15th nome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, were buried. Several tombs were destroyed by ancient quarrying and looting, some for building at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>, but their texts, read with quarry inscriptions at Hatnub, allow a family tree of the governors to be reconstructed.</p><p>The tomb of Djehutihotep, No. 2, was the most decorated of the ten recorded in 1891–1892. It has a portico with two columns with palm leaf capitals, an outer and an inner chamber, and a shrine. The colossus scene fills five of the seven rows of paintings on the left-hand wall of the inner chamber, showing the statue dragged to a temple and the sacrifices made on the occasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The cemetery was in use before the Middle Kingdom: rock-circle tombs of the Third Dynasty, the largest of their time yet found, lie west of the Middle Kingdom cemetery, and burials continued to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. Coptic monks later lived in the area, especially in the tombs of the valley. In 1891–1892 Percy E. Newberry, George Willoughby Fraser, Howard Carter and Marcus Worsley Blackden recorded the Middle Kingdom tombs for the Egypt Exploration Fund, and in 1897 and 1900 Georges Daressy and Ahmed Kamal found decorated coffins of Twelfth Dynasty officials.</p><p>In 1915 George Andrew Reisner's expedition for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> and the Museum of Fine Arts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, found the tomb of the nomarch Djehutynakht, whose painted coffins and wooden models are now in Boston. Work resumed in 1988 under Harco Willems, first for Leiden University and later for KU Leuven. In 2007 it found the burial chamber of Henu, an estate manager of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, with a ka-statue and wooden models, and in 2019 the oldest copy of the Book of Two Ways was announced, on the coffin of a woman named Ankh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deir El Bersha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early Middle Kingdom powerful provincial governors, the nomarchs, ruled their nomes with near-royal display, as the rock-cut tombs at Beni Hasan and the scene of a colossal statue being hauled in the tomb of Djehutyhotep at <b>Deir el-Bersha</b> show.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
